iv></div></div></a></figure></div><p>I have a team of people working on age=
ntic coding - working with AI to build software, which, I hear, is all the =
rage now. One frustration I had with the team this week was that nothing ev=
er seems to get =E2=80=9Cdone=E2=80=9D. Lots of tools, lots of one-offs, bu=
t nothing I would consider =E2=80=9Cshipping software=E2=80=9D. Then I real=
ized - that=E2=80=99s wrong. I=E2=80=99m mapping to the old paradigm, which=
 is common with a shift like this. Let=E2=80=99s think in terms of the new =
one. </p><p>AI can create a lot of software now. If you think if this as an=
 all or nothing =E2=80=9Cit=E2=80=99s shipped and done, or its junk=E2=80=
=9D, you=E2=80=99re missing the point. Complaining that a team is building =
a lot of software they don=E2=80=99t ship, is a bit like complaining they w=
rite a shopping list for themselves each week but never publish it as a boo=
k. That=E2=80=99s not the point of a shopping list, and it=E2=80=99s not th=
e point of most software right now. </p><p>Software is fundamentally a medi=
ation between intention and action. Until recently, that mediation was diff=
icult to create - it involved programmers, complex tools, long cycles. Ther=
e was scarcity because of this, so it made sense to =E2=80=9Caggregate=E2=
=80=9D that intention into roughly equivalent blocks (=E2=80=9Cgames=E2=80=
=9D, =E2=80=9Cproductivity=E2=80=9D, =E2=80=9Cfinancial=E2=80=9D etc), and =
then build once for as many people as possible. Software has a nice propert=
y of low marginal cost, so the game was really straightforward: invest to b=
uild something the largest number of people wanted, then ship the heck out =
of it. </p><p>That shifted a little when services replaced desktop apps, an=
d has continued to shift as it=E2=80=99s been easier and easier to create a=
nd distribute software - we can focus on narrower and smaller pieces of int=
ention, and the instant nature of services means we can iterate and focus i=
n on our audience. </p><p>AI based software reduces that friction even furt=
her, but it also changes the cost dynamic. It doesn=E2=80=99t make as much =
sense to build for everyone - it=E2=80=99s very easy now to create somethin=
g for your narrow intention, in the moment, and then discard it after. So t=
he bundling that=E2=80=99s the first part of that makes less sense. It also=
 costs to distribute software now (inference cost). So even if you can buil=
d something broad, it has to have direct economic benefit for anyone using =
it, for the costs to make sense - we have margin now. </p><p>Those two pres=
sures and changes to the friction of software creation mean the model and p=
aradigm have completely changed. There will still be software that ships, j=
ust like there are still books - there are mass audiences, and it can make =
sense to serve them. But there is also a vast new set of intentions and nee=
ds that can be served transiently - shopping lists, if you will. </p><p><sp=
an>So what do software professionals build? There aren=E2=80=99t any profes=
sional shopping list builders (well, probably a few, but not many). The rea=
l constraint now, the thing that is scarce, and that will always be scarce =
is </span><strong>human attention</strong><span>. Each of us only has a cer=
tain amount of time during the day to pay attention. You can optimize a bit=
 but you can=E2=80=99t fundamentally change it much - you can get leverage =
from what you pay attention to (which is why people have staff) and you can=
 be disciplined about not wasting time, but fundamentally, you still only h=
ave a human brain to pay attention with. </span></p><p><span>That means the=
re=E2=80=99s a high premium on low-maintenance </span><strong>building bloc=
ks</strong><span>. If you build something for a broad audience, it=E2=80=99=
s not very helpful if it increases their attentional load. It has to be som=
ething that has a clear job to do, does it well, doesn=E2=80=99t need care =
and feeding, and gets along well with others. </span></p><p>I think you can=
 see a bit of this emerging in what the beginnings of an operating system f=
or agents might be. Humans need features and hand-holding - we build comple=
x software for them. Agents don=E2=80=99t care - they need clarity and effi=
ciency. So simple, well-defined building blocks like github, markdown, html=
, yaml, python, rust, go, etc seem to be getting used more successfully. I =
built a presentation site for agents out of just html and git - not as full=
 featured as a presentation application but much easier for it to use. I ha=
ve a tool on my desktop that someone on my team built, called =E2=80=9CAtte=
ntion Firewall=E2=80=9D. It just watches background notifications and does =
filtering - much easier for it than integration with the dozen apps feeding=
 into those notifications - but that would never work for human software. <=
/p><p>Our challenge as software builders now is to get our minds into the n=
ew paradigm. It=E2=80=99s common to pattern match to the old paradigm when =
there is a shift like this. That can be useful in some ways, but it=E2=80=
=99s also urgent to recognize what has changed, and to keep our eye on what=
 is actually urgent and constant across the change. </p><div data-component=
